          forgotten. The one insight that could change the roadmap sits in a
          slide deck three teams away from anyone who could act on it.
        </p>
        <div class="story-reveal">
          <p>
            The problem was never listening. It's the distance between hearing
            something and doing something about it—the loop that never quite
            closes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every conversation, ticket, and review carries a signal. Left alone
            it's just noise. Connected to an owner and a decision, it becomes the
            roadmap your customers are already writing for you.
          </p>
          <p>
            The teams pulling ahead treat feedback as infrastructure. They route
            insight to whoever can act, close the loop in days instead of
            quarters, and earn a little more trust with every release they ship.
          </p>
          <p>
            That's the shift Loopwise is built for—from collecting feedback to
            closing loops, from reporting the past to deciding what happens next.
          </p>
          <p>
            When the loop closes fast, customers notice. That's how feedback
            quietly turns into loyalty.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ FEATURE COMPARISON (sticky nav + stacked cards) ============ -->
